  "text": "Here's the corrected implementation of the `remove_adjacent_duplicates` function, along with the test class.\n\n### \u2705 Description:\nThis function removes consecutive **duplicate elements** from a list. That is, if two or more identical elements are adjacent, they are reduced to just one occurrence.\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Solution:\n\n```python\nimport unittest\n\ndef remove_adjacent_duplicates(lst):\n    if not lst:\n        return []\n    \n    result = [lst[0]]  # Start with the first element\n    \n    for num in lst[1:]:\n        if num != result[-1]:\n            result.append(num)\n    \n    return result\n\nclass TestRemoveAdjacentDuplicates(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_basic_functionality(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4]), [1, 2, 3, 4])\n    \n    def test_repeated_elements(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([5, 5, 5, 6, 7, 7]), [5, 6, 7])\n    \n    def test_multiple_duplicates(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 3, 4, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5]), [1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    unittest.main()\n```\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 How It Works:\n\n- We initialize the result list with the first element of the input list.\n- Then, for each subsequent element in the input list, we compare it with the last element in the result list:\n  - If they are **not equal**, we add the new element to the result.\n  - If they are **equal**, we **skip** it (this is how we remove consecutive duplicates).\n- This ensures that only **non-consecutive duplicates** are kept.\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Example:\n\nInput: `[1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4]`  \nOutput: `[1, 2, 3, 4]`\n\n---\n\nLet me know if you want to support **non-consecutive duplicates** (i.e., remove *all* duplicates, not just adjacent ones) \u2014 that's a **different** problem!",
